WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.380
Drink a litre of water quickly, and your plasma sodium concentration dips for a

2
00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.480
while. No sodium left your body.

3
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:13.280
Sweat out a litre, and it rises, though sweat carries sodium out.

4
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:15.560
Why?

5
00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:24.597
Take a 70 kg adult with 42 litres of body water, 14 of them outside the cells.

6
00:00:26.257 --> 00:00:30.697
Cells hold their water mainly with potassium, the fluid outside mainly with sodium.

7
00:00:31.817 --> 00:00:38.097
Plasma sodium sits at 140 mmol per litre: a ratio, solute over water.

8
00:00:39.497 --> 00:00:44.657
Water crosses cell membranes easily, so a litre you drink spreads through all 43

9
00:00:44.657 --> 00:00:45.657
litres.

10
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:50.620
With the same solute, the concentration falls to about 137.

11
00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.140
Only about a third of a litre stays outside the cells.

12
00:00:56.283 --> 00:00:58.223
Sweat is far weaker than plasma.

13
00:00:59.603 --> 00:01:03.003
Its sodium varies widely; take 50 mmol per litre.

14
00:01:04.343 --> 00:01:09.583
It removes water out of proportion to solute, so the concentration rises to about

15
00:01:09.583 --> 00:01:10.583
142.

16
00:01:11.883 --> 00:01:13.563
A loop guards this ratio.

17
00:01:15.063 --> 00:01:19.823
Osmoreceptor neurons in the lamina terminalis, at the front of the brain, sense it.

18
00:01:21.003 --> 00:01:25.523
When plasma grows more concentrated, water leaves them, and they fire faster.

19
00:01:26.633 --> 00:01:28.953
Their signal raises vasopressin.

20
00:01:30.073 --> 00:01:33.853
In the collecting duct, vasopressin binds V2 receptors.

21
00:01:35.373 --> 00:01:39.513
Aquaporin-2 water channels move into the membrane facing the urine.

22
00:01:40.813 --> 00:01:42.173
Water returns to the blood.

23
00:01:43.183 --> 00:01:47.763
Without the hormone, urine can fall near 50 mOsm per kg.

24
00:01:49.023 --> 00:01:52.383
Short of water, it reaches about 1,000 to 1,200.

25
00:01:53.663 --> 00:01:57.743
The same neurons drive thirst, which replaces water already lost.

26
00:01:58.867 --> 00:02:00.367
Here is the one idea.

27
00:02:01.687 --> 00:02:07.367
In a healthy adult, plasma sodium concentration is body solute divided by body

28
00:02:07.367 --> 00:02:13.227
water. The water loop holds that ratio steady by adding or removing water.

29
00:02:14.447 --> 00:02:16.487
So the concentration reports water.

30
00:02:17.567 --> 00:02:23.327
Let the body keep an extra 140 mmol of sodium, about 8 g of salt.

31
00:02:24.607 --> 00:02:27.547
Once the water loop catches up, where does the gauge settle?

32
00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:39.880
Back at 140. The water loop holds back water until the ratio is restored.

33
00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:47.140
At that strength, 140 mmol fills one litre, all outside the cells, where sodium

34
00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:48.140
stays.

35
00:02:48.733 --> 00:02:52.293
So sodium content sets the volume outside the cells.

36
00:02:53.493 --> 00:02:55.073
A second loop guards it.

37
00:02:55.973 --> 00:03:00.593
It senses how well the arteries are filled, and sets how much sodium the kidney

38
00:03:00.593 --> 00:03:01.593
excretes.

39
00:03:02.167 --> 00:03:03.747
Two loops, two quantities.

40
00:03:05.307 --> 00:03:10.247
Water fixes the concentration; sodium, carrying its water, fixes the volume.

41
00:03:11.567 --> 00:03:16.187
A low sodium reading means too much water for the solute, with body sodium low,

42
00:03:16.567 --> 00:03:17.407
normal or high.
